ROOT=Path(__file__).parent; OUT=ROOT/'dist'; A=OUT/'assets'; A.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
def esc(x):return html.escape(str(x),quote=True)
def slug(x):return re.sub('[^a-z0-9]+','-',x.lower()).strip('-')
TAROT=[
('The Fool','Begin with curiosity','A new chapter does not require a perfect plan. Take one small, considered step toward something that makes you feel awake.','Pause before you leap. Curiosity works best when it travels with care.','Let a conversation surprise you.','Try a small experiment before a large commitment.'),
('The Magician','Use what is already yours','Your attention, skills, and experience are useful tools. Choose one intention and give it a practical place in your day.','Scattered effort can hide real ability. Simplify your next move.','Say what you mean with warmth.','Put one skill to work on a concrete task.'),
('The High Priestess','Make room to listen','Quiet can reveal what constant activity keeps covered. Notice your first feeling, then give yourself time to reflect before deciding.','Check whether silence is insight or avoidance. Ask for facts when you need them.','Listen without filling every pause.','Leave room for observation before a decision.'),
('The Empress','Care for what is growing','Something in your life may need nourishment rather than pressure. Offer steady attention to a relationship, an idea, or your own daily rhythms.','Giving without replenishing can leave you tired. Make your own needs part of the plan.','Care can be simple and specific.','Build conditions that support your creativity.'),
('The Emperor','Create a steady foundation','Helpful structure gives you room to breathe. Choose a boundary or routine that supports what matters without making life unnecessarily rigid.','Control can become exhausting. Make room for flexibility inside your plan.','A clear boundary can be an act of care.','Define responsibilities before adding more work.'),
('The Hierophant','Choose your own wisdom','Tradition can offer a starting point without providing every answer. Keep what supports your values and gently question what no longer fits.','A familiar rule may deserve a second look. You can learn from others without surrendering judgment.','Talk about the values you share.','Seek guidance from someone whose approach you respect.'),
('The Lovers','Choose with intention','A meaningful choice begins with honesty about what matters. Look for alignment between your actions, your needs, and the relationships you want to cultivate.','Mixed priorities may need a direct conversation. Return to your own values.','Ask for mutual understanding, not mind reading.','Compare an opportunity with your actual priorities.'),
('The Chariot','Move with purpose','Progress can be quiet and deliberate. Decide where your energy belongs, then give your next step a clear direction.','Momentum is not the same as alignment. Slow down long enough to check the route.','Move toward a shared goal at a pace that suits both people.','Choose one priority and protect time for it.'),
('Strength','Let gentleness lead','Steadiness does not always look forceful. A patient response may give you more room to act than a struggle for immediate control.','You do not have to push through every feeling. Rest can support your resolve.','Be tender without giving up your boundaries.','Approach a difficult task in manageable parts.'),
('The Hermit','Find your own quiet','Step back from the noise for a moment. A little space may help you distinguish what you want from what others expect.','Solitude can become isolation. Consider a conversation with someone you trust.','Ask for space clearly and kindly.','Set aside uninterrupted time to reflect.'),
('Wheel of Fortune','Meet the changing season','Some circumstances shift beyond your control. Focus on the choices that remain yours and stay open to adjusting your plans.','A delay can invite a different approach. Avoid treating uncertainty as a verdict.','Let a relationship evolve through honest conversation.','Keep a flexible plan for changing circumstances.'),
('Justice','Look at the whole picture','Bring fairness and clear thinking to the question in front of you. Consider what happened, what you know, and what responsibility belongs to you.','A quick judgment may miss important context. Revisit the facts.','Make room for both perspectives.','Document expectations and ask clear questions.'),
('The Hanged Man','Try another perspective','You may not need to solve everything today. A thoughtful pause can reveal an angle that urgency makes difficult to see.','Waiting without intention can become avoidance. Decide what would make the pause useful.','Consider how the situation looks to the other person.','Reframe the problem before changing the plan.'),
('Death','Make room for change','This card reflects endings and transition, not a prediction of physical death. Consider what you are ready to release so something more fitting can take its place.','Holding on can be understandable and tiring. Let change happen in small, supported steps.','Name what needs to change without assuming the outcome.','Retire a process that no longer serves its purpose.'),
('Temperance','Find a gentler rhythm','Balance often grows through adjustment rather than perfection. Combine patience with action and notice the pace you can actually sustain.','Too many demands may be pulling in different directions. Restore one simple rhythm.','Allow different needs to share the conversation.','Build recovery time into your schedule.'),
('The Devil','Notice what holds you','Look kindly at a habit or expectation that feels limiting. Awareness can create a little more choice where things have started to feel automatic.','A pattern may be loosening its grip. Support change with practical help and clear boundaries.','Notice the difference between connection and obligation.','Question an assumption that keeps you stuck.'),
('The Tower','Return to what is solid','When an assumption changes, it can feel unsettling. Focus on practical support and the next manageable step rather than trying to explain everything at once.','Resisting a necessary adjustment can use precious energy. Approach it with support.','Choose honest, grounded conversation.','Review the foundations of a plan before rebuilding.'),
('The Star','Leave a little room for hope','You do not need certainty to care for your future. Let a small restorative action remind you that possibility and patience can exist together.','Hope may feel distant when you are depleted. Begin with care, not pressure.','Offer openness at a pace that feels safe.','Reconnect with the purpose behind your work.'),
('The Moon','Walk gently through uncertainty','Not every feeling is a fact, and not every question needs an immediate answer. Notice what is unclear and seek information before drawing conclusions.','Something may be becoming clearer. Check your interpretation against what you can observe.','Ask instead of assuming.','Separate evidence from worries before acting.'),
('The Sun','Notice what feels alive','Give your attention to a source of honest enjoyment. Simple moments of warmth can help you recognize what deserves more space in your life.','Enjoyment does not need to look impressive. Let small good moments count.','Make time for uncomplicated connection.','Notice the tasks that bring out your energy.'),
('Judgement','Answer your own invitation','Reflection can lead to a fresh choice without turning the past into a punishment. Consider what you have learned and how you want to respond now.','Self criticism may be louder than insight. Try a more useful and compassionate question.','Let learning guide the next conversation.','Review experience for lessons rather than blame.'),
('The World','Recognize what has come together','A cycle may be ready for acknowledgment. Take stock of your effort, appreciate what you have learned, and allow a little space before the next beginning.','An unfinished detail may need attention. Completion can be quiet and imperfect.','Acknowledge the ways a connection has grown.','Close a project thoughtfully before opening another.')]
CARDS=[dict(name=x[0],slug=slug(x[0]),theme=x[1],message=x[2],reverse=x[3],love=x[4],career=x[5],number=i) for i,x in enumerate(TAROT)]
SIGNS=[('Aries','♈','March 21 – April 19','Fire','Begin thoughtfully','03-21'),('Taurus','♉','April 20 – May 20','Earth','Return to simple comforts','04-20'),('Gemini','♊','May 21 – June 20','Air','Follow a useful question','05-21'),('Cancer','♋','June 21 – July 22','Water','Make space for your needs','06-21'),('Leo','♌','July 23 – August 22','Fire','Create without an audience','07-23'),('Virgo','♍','August 23 – September 22','Earth','Let enough be enough','08-23'),('Libra','♎','September 23 – October 22','Air','Look for mutual understanding','09-23'),('Scorpio','♏','October 23 – November 21','Water','Be honest with yourself','10-23'),('Sagittarius','♐','November 22 – December 21','Fire','Explore a new perspective','11-22'),('Capricorn','♑','December 22 – January 19','Earth','Build at a sustainable pace','12-22'),('Aquarius','♒','January 20 – February 18','Air','Make room for a different idea','01-20'),('Pisces','♓','February 19 – March 20','Water','Give imagination a place','02-19')]
ZODIAC=[dict(name=s[0],slug=s[0].lower(),icon=s[1],dates=s[2],element=s[3],theme=s[4],start=s[5]) for s in SIGNS]
ARTICLES=[
 dict(title='A quieter way to hear your intuition',category='Intuition',slug='a-quieter-way-to-hear-your-intuition',intro='You do not have to make your mind completely quiet to notice what matters. Begin with one unhurried moment.',parts=[('Start with what you notice','Intuition can be a useful name for a subtle impression, a remembered pattern, or a feeling you have not yet put into words. You do not need to treat that impression as an instruction. Begin by noticing it. Where were you when it appeared? What had just happened? What words describe it without turning it into a prediction? Writing a few ordinary sentences can help you see the experience more clearly.'),('Separate the feeling from the story','A feeling may be real even when the explanation attached to it is incomplete. You might feel uneasy before a conversation and assume the other person is upset. There could also be a simpler explanation: you are tired, the topic matters to you, or a previous experience is coloring the moment. Try writing two lines, one for what you feel and one for what you actually know. Leave room between them.'),('Build a small listening practice','Choose a time that already has a natural pause, perhaps while tea cools or before you open your laptop. Put your phone out of reach for a few minutes. Notice your breathing without trying to improve it. Ask yourself what needs attention today, then write the first answer and one practical question about it. The point is not to produce something profound. It is to become more familiar with your own patterns.'),('Let evidence join the conversation','Inner reflection works best alongside information, experience, and appropriate professional advice. If a choice has serious consequences, give yourself time to gather facts and talk with the right people. An impression can help you identify a question worth asking. It cannot verify another person’s motives, diagnose a condition, or guarantee an outcome. Keeping that distinction clear can make a reflective practice more useful and less pressured.'),('Try one gentle experiment','At the end of the day, look back at one impression you recorded. What did you learn? Did the feeling change after a meal, a walk, or an honest conversation? You are not grading your intuition. You are learning how context affects you. Over time, this practice may help you recognize the difference between a familiar worry and a concern that deserves closer examination. Stay curious rather than trying to prove yourself right.')]),
 dict(title='How to ask a thoughtful tarot question',category='Tarot',slug='how-to-ask-a-thoughtful-tarot-question',intro='The most useful question leaves room for your choices. A little preparation can make a reading feel more focused.',parts=[('Bring the situation into focus','Before you choose a question, describe the situation in a few plain sentences. What is happening now? What part feels unclear? What have you already tried? You do not need to tell the whole history or find a polished way to explain yourself. A simple description helps separate the question you want to explore from the many feelings that may be surrounding it.'),('Ask about your part','Questions about your own choices usually create more room for reflection than questions that ask a card to reveal someone else’s private thoughts. Instead of asking whether another person will change, consider asking what boundaries or conversations would help you. Instead of asking for an exact date, ask what preparation is within your control. This does not remove uncertainty, but it gives the conversation a practical place to begin.'),('Keep one question at the center','It is natural for one concern to lead to several others. Write them all down, then choose the one you most want to understand. If your question includes several separate decisions, try dividing it into smaller parts. A focused question can still be open and generous. You might ask what deserves attention in a career transition, or what you could learn from a recurring pattern in your relationships.'),('Allow the answer to be reflective','Tarot is a symbolic tool for personal reflection and entertainment. A card does not establish facts about other people or replace evidence. During a reading, notice which ideas feel useful and which do not fit. You are allowed to ask for clarification, disagree with an interpretation, or leave a question open. The value of the conversation can be in the perspective it offers rather than a promise about what happens next.'),('Take away one useful action','After the reading, write down one idea you want to consider and one action you can take without depending on a prediction. That could be arranging a conversation, researching an option, or simply allowing yourself time. Avoid making a large decision only because a card appeared. Let the reading sit alongside your own judgment and the relevant facts. A thoughtful question is a beginning, and your choices remain yours.')]),
 dict(title='Making space for an honest conversation',category='Love',slug='making-space-for-an-honest-conversation',intro='Connection often begins with something smaller than the perfect words: enough space to listen.',parts=[('Choose a workable moment','A difficult conversation can feel even harder when either person is rushed, distracted, or exhausted. Ask whether it is a reasonable time to talk and be specific about what you hope to discuss. If it is not a good moment, agree on another time when possible. This small act of preparation can make the conversation feel less like an ambush and more like an invitation to understand each other.'),('Start with what you have observed','Describe an event you both could recognize before explaining the meaning you have attached to it. There is a difference between saying a message went unanswered and saying someone does not care. The first creates room for information. The second may turn a guess into an accusation. You can still be clear about how the situation affected you without claiming to know the other person’s intentions.'),('Make your request clear','A need can be hard to respond to when it stays hidden inside a complaint. Consider what a useful change would look like in ordinary behavior. Do you want advance notice, uninterrupted time, a clearer agreement, or permission to take a pause? Ask directly and leave room for the other person to respond. A request is not a guarantee that you will agree, but it gives the conversation something concrete to work with.'),('Listen for the meaning','Listening does not require accepting every interpretation. It means trying to understand what the other person is saying before preparing your reply. You might repeat the main point in your own words and ask if you understood it correctly. Notice when you are reacting to a tone, an old memory, or a fear rather than the words in front of you. A short pause can help both people regain their footing.'),('Keep your boundaries in view','Honest conversation is not a requirement to tolerate intimidation or harm. You can pause, leave, or seek support when a conversation feels unsafe. In everyday disagreements, clear boundaries can also make connection easier by naming what you can and cannot offer. Reflection or a reading may help you organize your thoughts, but it cannot establish another person’s motives or replace appropriate support. Take care of your own wellbeing as you decide what comes next.')]),
 dict(title='A simple ritual for the changing moon',category='Moon',slug='a-simple-ritual-for-the-changing-moon',intro='Let the moon be a reminder to pause, reflect, and begin again. No special tools are required.',parts=[('Use the sky as a reminder','The moon’s changing appearance offers a visible rhythm that many people enjoy using for reflection. You do not have to believe it controls your life to find that rhythm meaningful. A calendar reminder or a brief look outside can be enough. The practice is about creating a recurring moment to notice your own experience, not about predicting events or assigning a cause to every change in mood.'),('Choose a small intention','At the start of your reflection, ask what you would like to give attention to in the coming days. Keep the answer within your influence. You might choose to make time for rest, finish a neglected task, or have a conversation you have been postponing. Write the intention as a gentle direction rather than a demand. A useful intention gives you room to respond to real circumstances instead of judging yourself for an imperfect week.'),('Notice what is already present','As the month continues, return to the intention and notice what has happened. What helped? What made it harder? Is the intention still relevant? You do not need to force the same plan through every change. Reflection can reveal that something smaller would be more sustainable or that a different priority now needs care. Treat the notes as information rather than a record of success or failure.'),('Release a little pressure','A symbolic release can be as simple as writing down an expectation and deciding not to carry it into the next week. There is no need for fire, special purchases, or an elaborate ritual. You could fold the paper and set it aside, take a short walk, or close your notebook with a deliberate breath. The gesture does not change circumstances by itself. It marks a choice about where you want to place your attention.'),('Make the practice your own','If observing the moon starts to feel like another obligation, simplify it. A few lines once a month may suit you better than daily tracking. You can share the practice with a friend or keep it private. Let curiosity guide you, and remember that seasonal and personal rhythms vary. The most useful ritual is one that leaves you feeling more connected to your own experience, with enough flexibility to keep living outside the notebook.')]),
 dict(title='Preparing for your first reading',category='Tarot',slug='preparing-for-your-first-reading',intro='You can arrive with questions, uncertainty, or a little of both. Here is how to make room for the experience.',parts=[('Know what you are booking','Read the description of the format before you choose. A video reading is a scheduled conversation, a chat reading happens through typed messages, and an email reading gives you written material to revisit. Consider how you prefer to communicate and how much time you can comfortably set aside. Confirm the price, timing, and cancellation terms before paying. On this demonstration site, those business details are placeholders and no real appointment is reserved.'),('Write down what matters','Bring one or two topics you would like to explore. A question can be simple: what deserves attention in a transition, what you could learn from a pattern, or how you might approach a conversation. Avoid putting pressure on yourself to reveal more than feels comfortable. You can say that a topic is sensitive and decide which details are relevant. A focused starting point can help make the time feel purposeful.'),('Prepare your space','For a live session, choose somewhere you can hear or type without frequent interruption. Check your device, internet connection, and scheduled time zone. Keep water and something to write with nearby if that suits you. An email reading may need less preparation, but it still helps to write your questions when you have a little time to think. You do not need candles, crystals, or a special setting to take part.'),('Keep your own judgment','A reading offers a perspective for reflection and entertainment. It cannot verify hidden facts, guarantee outcomes, or replace medical, legal, financial, or mental health advice. You can ask what an interpretation means, say when something does not resonate, and decline a topic. A useful experience leaves room for your agency. There is no obligation to make a decision immediately because an idea came up during the conversation.'),('Leave time afterward','If possible, give yourself a few quiet minutes after the session. Record what stood out and what you want to think about further. Separate observations from predictions and identify any practical action that makes sense on its own merits. You might revisit your notes after a day and find that some ideas matter more than others. Let the experience settle at a natural pace. Your next step can be modest, considered, and entirely your own.')]),
 dict(title='Small ways to come back to yourself',category='Self-care',slug='small-ways-to-come-back-to-yourself',intro='A reset does not have to become a project. Sometimes the helpful thing is the smallest thing you can actually do.',parts=[('Notice the basics first','When a day feels scattered, begin with ordinary needs. Have you eaten, had water, moved a little, or taken a break from a screen? These questions are not a complete answer to every difficult feeling, but they can offer a practical starting point. Choose something available and manageable. You do not need to earn a pause by finishing everything first, and you do not need to turn care into another standard to meet.'),('Make one place a little calmer','Pick a small area within reach, perhaps a desk corner, bedside table, or favorite chair. Move what is distracting and leave something useful nearby. The goal is not a perfect room or an impressive routine. It is to create one place where your attention has fewer demands. If tidying feels like too much, simply changing your position or opening a curtain may be enough for the moment.'),('Give the feeling a name','Try describing how you feel in specific, ordinary language. Tired, disappointed, uncertain, and overstimulated suggest different needs. You can acknowledge a feeling without solving it or making it your identity. Writing a sentence may help, or you might say it quietly to yourself. If naming it brings more questions, that is fine. The exercise is a way to notice your experience, not a test with a correct answer.'),('Choose a kind boundary','Sometimes care means removing one demand rather than adding a new activity. You might postpone a nonessential task, mute notifications for a short period, or be honest about what you can offer. A boundary can be clear without a long defense. Consider what is realistic in your circumstances and communicate it when needed. Small adjustments can protect a little attention while leaving room for the responsibilities you still want or need to carry.'),('Let support be part of the picture','You do not have to make every difficult day manageable by yourself. A trusted person, a community resource, or a qualified professional may provide support that a private ritual cannot. Reflective tools can help you organize thoughts, but they are not a replacement for appropriate care. Start with what is possible today. A useful reset might be a conversation, a glass of water, or permission to stop asking so much of this particular moment.')])]
FAQ=[('Readings','How do video readings work?','Choose a duration and a time, then connect from a quiet space using your device. In this demo, the video room is a preview only. A real meeting provider must be connected before launch.'),('Readings','How does a chat reading work?','A scheduled chat reading is a private typed conversation. The floating chat on this site is a scripted demo assistant for questions about the website, not a reading with Amy.'),('Readings','When will I receive my email reading?','The proposed delivery window is 48 hours after purchase. The demo confirmation calculates an exact delivery time. [Amy to confirm the delivery promise before launch.]'),('Privacy','Is my reading confidential?','[Amy to confirm confidentiality and retention policies.] This demo saves booking details only in your browser. Do not enter sensitive personal information.'),('Booking','Can I reschedule?','Yes, the demo account lets you choose a new date and time for a saved booking. [Amy to confirm the notice period and any rescheduling fee.]'),('Payments','What payment methods do you accept?','Checkout currently simulates a card payment. No money is collected. [Connect and confirm accepted payment methods with Amy before launch.]'),('Readings','Do I need to prepare anything?','Bring an open question and a quiet moment. For video, check your connection and camera. You can share only what you are comfortable sharing.'),('Booking','Which time zone will my appointment use?','The calendar displays your selected time zone. Availability is modeled from Central Time in Red Wing, Minnesota, with daylight saving changes taken into account.'),('Payments','Can I use a gift reading?','Yes. Complete a demo gift purchase to create a gift code, then redeem it in the checkout promo field on this browser. No real gift value is issued.'),('Account','How do I sign in to the demo?','Use demo@amyreadings.com and demo1234. Demo accounts and bookings stay in this browser. They are not secure production accounts.'),('Account','Can I reset my password?','The reset form demonstrates the confirmation screen. It does not send email or change a real password.'),('Payments','What is the refund policy?','[Amy to supply the refund policy.] No money is collected in the demo. Please read the Refund Policy page before a real purchase.'),('Booking','Can readings guarantee an outcome?','No. Readings are for entertainment and personal insight. They cannot guarantee events or replace professional advice.')]
